-- Chess, used here purely as a teaching example. Every other example in
-- this repo is a real business domain; this one is deliberately a game,
-- because chess makes a few ideas easy to see.
--
-- One idea: some rules are about what a board position is allowed
-- to look like, while others are about how the board is allowed to
-- *change* from one move to the next. "At most one king per colour" is
-- the first kind - you can check it by looking at a single snapshot of
-- the board. But "every move advances the move counter by exactly one",
-- "the turn always passes to the other player", and "a move never
-- adds a piece and removes at most one" are the second kind. You cannot check them by looking
-- at one position alone; you have to compare the board before the move
-- with the board after it. That is what makes them interesting.
--
-- Morpholog expresses the second kind with `pre(...)`. Read
-- `pre(MoveCount(m))` as "the move count as it was before this move";
-- the unwrapped `MoveCount(n)` is the value after. A rule that mentions
-- both can constrain how one becomes the other.
--
-- Another idea: a square has a colour, and that colour is stored
-- nowhere - it is *computed* from where the square sits. We name squares
-- by two numbers, the file (column, 1 to 8) and the rank (row, 1 to 8),
-- so the square a chess player calls c1 is file 3, rank 1. A square is
-- dark when file + rank is even and light when it is odd - that is all
-- "colour" means on a chessboard. We write the test as `(file + rank) %
-- 2`, where `%` is the remainder after division: an even sum leaves
-- remainder 0 (call it dark), an odd sum leaves remainder 1 (light).
-- From that one piece of arithmetic comes a well-known property we can
-- enforce - the two bishops of a colour always stand on
-- opposite-coloured squares.
--
-- The terms used below. An `invariant` is a rule that must hold for all
-- admitted state - the runtime refuses to commit any change that would
-- break one. A `require` is a gate on a single move, checked at the
-- moment the move is made. And the runtime stores `claims` it has agreed
-- to admit, never bare "facts": the board is just a set of claims that
-- such-and-such piece sits on such-and-such square.
--
-- This is not a chess engine. Beyond the rules written here it does not
-- enforce legal moves (a rook may leap, a bishop may slide sideways),
-- and it knows nothing of check, castling, or promotion. It models only
-- enough to show before-and-after rules and square colour. Inspired by
-- Murat Demirbas's Chess invariants post:
-- https://muratbuffalo.blogspot.com/2026/05/chess-invariants.html

program chess_transition_invariants

-- The vocabulary. The board is just a pile of "this piece is on this
-- square" claims, where a square is its file and rank (each 1 to 8),
-- plus two claims about the game as a whole: how many moves have been
-- played, and whose turn it is. Nothing else is stored. How many pieces
-- are left, for one, is never written down: the rules count it from
-- the board whenever they need it, so it can never disagree with the
-- board.
-- One piece per square, declared on the predicate: the coordinates
-- determine the occupant.
predicate PieceAt(file: Decimal, rank: Decimal, piece_type: Subject, color: Subject)
    unique by (file, rank)
predicate MoveCount(n: Decimal)
predicate CurrentTurn(color: Subject)

-- Intents are messages announced to the outside world after a move
-- commits: the game started, a piece moved. A move is announced by the
-- squares it ran between; whether it took a piece is on the board.
intent GameStarted()
intent PieceMoved(src_file: Decimal, src_rank: Decimal, dst_file: Decimal, dst_rank: Decimal)

-- Rules about a single board position. Each one can be judged by
-- looking at one snapshot; none needs to know what the board looked
-- like a move ago.

-- Every piece stands on a real square: file and rank are whole numbers
-- from 1 to 8. The bishop rule below depends on this. On file 3.5 its
-- colour formula gives 0.5, neither dark nor light, so a bishop there
-- would never match its partner's colour and would always pass.
-- `1 <= file <= 8` reads as spoken, both comparisons at once, and
-- `file % 1 = 0` says the number leaves no remainder when divided by
-- one: it is whole.
invariant pieces_stand_on_the_board:
  PieceAt(file: file, rank: rank, ..)
  implies (1 <= file <= 8 and 1 <= rank <= 8 and file % 1 = 0 and rank % 1 = 0)

-- Exactly one king of each colour - always. `sum(1 | PieceAt(_, _,
-- #king, #white))` counts how many white-king claims are on the board
-- (it adds 1 for every match), and we insist that count is 1. This is
-- stronger than "at most one king": it also forbids the count dropping
-- to zero, so a king can never be captured. We state it once per colour,
-- since chess has exactly two.
invariant exactly_one_white_king:
  sum(1 | PieceAt(_, _, #king, #white)) = 1

invariant exactly_one_black_king:
  sum(1 | PieceAt(_, _, #king, #black)) = 1

-- The two bishops of a colour stand on opposite-coloured squares. A
-- square's colour is `(file + rank) % 2` - 0 for dark, 1 for light - so
-- whenever two *different* squares each hold a bishop of the same colour
-- `c`, their colours must differ. This is the well-known chessboard
-- property: a light-squared bishop and a dark-squared bishop. One
-- snapshot settles it, and a move that would land both bishops of a
-- colour on one colour is turned away. But the rule needs two bishops
-- to compare. Once one is captured, it has nothing to compare, and the
-- survivor could change colour without this rule noticing.
-- (Note `c` is bound by the first bishop and reused in the second, so
-- the two are the same colour; the `not (...)` excludes comparing a
-- bishop with itself.)
--
-- The `let` lines name the colour formula before the rule uses it.
-- Nothing is stored, and nothing runs early: the rule means exactly
-- what it would mean with the formula written out at each use, so
-- `f_a` and the rest mean the squares the two bishop claims match.
-- The names are for the reader.
invariant bishops_on_opposite_square_colors:
  let colour_a = ((f_a + r_a) % 2)
  let colour_b = ((f_b + r_b) % 2)
  (PieceAt(f_a, r_a, #bishop, c) and PieceAt(f_b, r_b, #bishop, c) and not (f_a = f_b and r_a = r_b))
  implies (colour_a != colour_b)

-- At most eight pawns per colour. Here `c` is bound by the left side to
-- whichever colour has a pawn, and the count on the right is scoped to
-- that same colour. (Pawns only ever leave the board in this model, so
-- the bound is never really threatened - it is here to show counting
-- inside a comparison.)
invariant at_most_eight_pawns_per_color:
  PieceAt(_, _, #pawn, c) implies (sum(1 | PieceAt(_, _, #pawn, c)) <= 8)

-- Rules about how the board changes from one move to the next. These
-- compare the board after the move (the plain reading) with the board
-- before it (the `pre(...)` reading). You could not state them by
-- looking at a single position.

-- Every move bumps the move counter up by exactly one. The new count
-- must equal the previous count plus one.
invariant move_count_strictly_increases:
  (MoveCount(n) and pre(MoveCount(m))) implies (n = (m + 1))

-- The turn always flips: whoever is to move now must be the opposite
-- colour to whoever was to move before. The move below works the next
-- turn out itself; this rule checks the result from the outside, so a
-- future move that forgets cannot slip through.
invariant turn_alternates:
  (CurrentTurn(t_now) and pre(CurrentTurn(t_prev))) implies (t_now != t_prev)

-- A move never adds a piece, and the board loses at most one. Count
-- the pieces after the move, and count them again on the board as it
-- was before: the first count is the same as the second (a quiet move)
-- or one less (a capture). `pre(...)` works inside a count too:
-- `sum(1 | pre(PieceAt(..)))` counts the pieces before the move. The
-- rule starts once a game is under way, so setting up the 32 pieces is
-- not a "move".
--
-- It counts pieces; it does not follow them. So it does not say by
-- itself that only one piece was taken. That comes from `move_piece`
-- below, which removes only two squares' pieces: its own, and whatever
-- stood on the destination.
invariant piece_count_drops_by_at_most_one:
  let after = (sum(1 | PieceAt(..)))
  let before = (sum(1 | pre(PieceAt(..))))
  pre(MoveCount(_)) implies (before - 1 <= after <= before)

-- The ways the board may change. A move proposes a set of changes; the
-- runtime applies them only if everything they touch still obeys every
-- invariant. `admit` adds a claim, `retract` removes one, `require`
-- gates the move.

-- Set up the opening position: all 32 pieces on their starting squares,
-- the move counter at zero, white to play. Files
-- a to h are 1 to 8; the white army sits on ranks 1 and 2, the black
-- army on ranks 7 and 8. The gate refuses a game already under way:
-- starting it again would announce a second start of the same game.
transformation start_game():
  require game_not_yet_started: not MoveCount(_)
  admit PieceAt(1, 1, #rook, #white)
  admit PieceAt(2, 1, #knight, #white)
  admit PieceAt(3, 1, #bishop, #white)
  admit PieceAt(4, 1, #queen, #white)
  admit PieceAt(5, 1, #king, #white)
  admit PieceAt(6, 1, #bishop, #white)
  admit PieceAt(7, 1, #knight, #white)
  admit PieceAt(8, 1, #rook, #white)
  admit PieceAt(1, 2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(2, 2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(3, 2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(4, 2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(5, 2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(6, 2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(7, 2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(8, 2, #pawn, #white)
  admit PieceAt(1, 7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(2, 7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(3, 7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(4, 7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(5, 7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(6, 7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(7, 7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(8, 7, #pawn, #black)
  admit PieceAt(1, 8, #rook, #black)
  admit PieceAt(2, 8, #knight, #black)
  admit PieceAt(3, 8, #bishop, #black)
  admit PieceAt(4, 8, #queen, #black)
  admit PieceAt(5, 8, #king, #black)
  admit PieceAt(6, 8, #bishop, #black)
  admit PieceAt(7, 8, #knight, #black)
  admit PieceAt(8, 8, #rook, #black)
  admit MoveCount(0)
  admit CurrentTurn(#white)
  emit GameStarted()

-- A move. The player says only which piece moves, by its square, and
-- where it goes. The referee works out the rest: whose turn comes next,
-- and whether the move takes a piece.
--
-- The gates come first. The piece must belong to the player whose turn
-- it is, and the destination must not hold one of that player's own
-- pieces.
--
-- Then the move says what the next position is. Whatever stood on the
-- destination is gone: `retract PieceAt(file: dst_f, rank: dst_r, ..)`
-- removes any piece on that square, and does nothing if the square is
-- empty. So one move covers both a quiet move and a capture, and the
-- player never has to say which it is. The piece leaves its own square
-- and lands on the destination, the move count goes up by one, and the
-- turn passes to the other player.
transformation move_piece(src_f, src_r, dst_f, dst_r):
  bind PieceAt(src_f, src_r, piece_type, piece_color)
  bind CurrentTurn(current_turn)
  bind MoveCount(m)
  require your_own_piece: piece_color = current_turn
  require not_onto_your_own_piece: not PieceAt(file: dst_f, rank: dst_r, color: current_turn, ..)
  let next_turn = if(current_turn = #white, #black, #white)
  let next_m = m + 1
  retract PieceAt(file: dst_f, rank: dst_r, ..)
  retract PieceAt(src_f, src_r, piece_type, piece_color)
  retract CurrentTurn(current_turn)
  retract MoveCount(m)
  admit PieceAt(dst_f, dst_r, piece_type, piece_color)
  admit CurrentTurn(next_turn)
  admit MoveCount(next_m)
  emit PieceMoved(src_f, src_r, dst_f, dst_r)
